        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-78",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "OS Command Injection in /api/tools/ping via host Parameter: - **Source**: Untrusted user input enters the endpoint via the JSON request payload field `host` (`data.get('host', '')`) on line 107.\n- **Sanitization/Validation**: The application only checks that the field is non-empty (`if not host:`). It performs no validation, whitelisting (e.g. IP address or hostname validation), or shell escaping.\n- **Sink**: The unvalidated string is formatted into `command = f\"ping -c 1 {host}\"` and executed directly via `os.system(command)` on line 114, which spawns a system shell (`/bin/sh -c`).\n- **Data Flow**: The client posts a JSON payload containing shell metacharacters -> `host` is extracted -> String interpolation constructs the shell command -> `os.system` executes the command with shell interpretation, executing attacker-supplied commands with the privileges of the web application process.\n\n**Conceptual Exploit Payload**:\nSend a POST request with command chaining operators (e.g., `;`, `&&`, or `|`) in the `host` parameter:\n```http\nPOST /api/tools/ping HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:5000\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"host\": \"127.0.0.1; cat /etc/passwd\"\n}\n```\nThe resulting shell execution will run `ping -c 1 127.0.0.1` followed by `cat /etc/passwd`."
