        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) in /api/documents/<doc_id>: - **Source**: Untrusted user input enters the endpoint via the URL route parameter `doc_id` (`/api/documents/<int:doc_id>`) on line 82 and the `X-User-ID` request header on line 84.\n- **Sanitization/Validation**: The application checks whether `X-User-ID` header is present (`if not current_user_id: return 401`), but fails to perform any authorization check or access control validation to ensure `current_user_id` matches the document's `owner_id`.\n- **Sink**: The document is retrieved solely by primary key (`WHERE id = ?`) without ownership verification and returned to the caller on line 98 (`return jsonify({\"status\": \"success\", \"data\": dict(doc)})`).\n- **Data Flow**: A user supplies an arbitrary `doc_id` in the request path alongside any non-empty `X-User-ID` header -> The endpoint queries the document strictly by ID -> The record is returned without validating that `doc['owner_id'] == int(current_user_id)`.\n\n**Conceptual Exploit Payload**:\nAn unprivileged user with user ID 2 (Alice) sends a request to retrieve document ID 1 (Admin Confidential document owned by user ID 1):\n```http\nGET /api/documents/1 HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:5000\nX-User-ID: 2\n```\nBecause authorization checking is missing, the endpoint responds with HTTP 200 and the admin document's title and contents (`\"Super Secret Admin Report\"`)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "app.py"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 92,
                  "endLine": 93
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) in /api/documents/<doc_id>: - **Source**: Untrusted user input enters the endpoint via the URL route parameter `doc_id` (`/api/documents/<int:doc_id>`) on line 82 and the `X-User-ID` request header on line 84.\n- **Sanitization/Validation**: The application checks whether `X-User-ID` header is present (`if not current_user_id: return 401`), but fails to perform any authorization check or access control validation to ensure `current_user_id` matches the document's `owner_id`.\n- **Sink**: The document is retrieved solely by primary key (`WHERE id = ?`) without ownership verification and returned to the caller on line 98 (`return jsonify({\"status\": \"success\", \"data\": dict(doc)})`).\n- **Data Flow**: A user supplies an arbitrary `doc_id` in the request path alongside any non-empty `X-User-ID` header -> The endpoint queries the document strictly by ID -> The record is returned without validating that `doc['owner_id'] == int(current_user_id)`.\n\n**Conceptual Exploit Payload**:\nAn unprivileged user with user ID 2 (Alice) sends a request to retrieve document ID 1 (Admin Confidential document owned by user ID 1):\n```http\nGET /api/documents/1 HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:5000\nX-User-ID: 2\n```\nBecause authorization checking is missing, the endpoint responds with HTTP 200 and the admin document's title and contents (`\"Super Secret Admin Report\"`)."
          },
